1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,900
You know the Giza pyramids. But almost nobody knows why Egypt never died after it lost its own kings for 2,300 years.

2
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:17,100
Persians, Greeks, Romans, Arabs, Turks, the British. Every one of those empires ruled Egypt, and every one of them is gone.

3
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:24,300
Egypt is still here. The answer is in this picture. By the end of this video, you'll know exactly how it worked.

4
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:32,000
That line is the Nile. Nearly everyone in Egypt lives along it, because almost everything else is desert.

5
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:39,400
Every summer, monsoon rains in the highlands of Ethiopia swelled the river. In Egypt, it flooded the valley

6
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:47,800
for about three months, and when the water pulled back, it left a layer of black, fertile mud. Egyptians named their country after it:

7
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:55,700
Kemet, the Black Land. The desert on both sides was Deshret, the Red Land. The Egyptian year was even built around the river.

8
00:00:55,700 --> 00:01:02,200
It had three seasons: Flood, Growing and Harvest. Farming, taxes and festivals all followed that cycle.

9
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:09,600
That mud gave Egypt something rare in the ancient world: a reliable surplus. Farmers grew far more grain than they needed,

10
00:01:09,600 --> 00:01:16,500
and that extra grain could feed builders, soldiers, priests and officials. It paid for everything you're about to see.

11
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:23,400
They measured the flood on stone stairways called Nilometers. A high flood meant big harvests and higher taxes.

12
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:29,900
A low one meant famine. Keep that in mind: everything in this story rises and falls with the river.

13
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:38,100
The river was also Egypt's highway. The current carried boats north, and the wind usually blows from the north, so boats could sail back south.

14
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:45,800
Moving grain, stone and armies along the valley was easy. And the deserts on either side worked like walls. For centuries,

15
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:53,500
they kept large armies out, so Egypt could grow rich without constantly defending itself. Around 3100 BC,

16
00:01:53,500 --> 00:02:00,400
a king from the south conquered the north and united Upper and Lower Egypt. Upper Egypt was the south,

17
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:08,300
because the Nile flows north. Egyptians remembered the founder as Menes; historians link him to a king called Narmer.

18
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:14,800
The capital, Memphis, sat where the valley meets the delta, so it could control both. Around the same time,

19
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:22,200
hieroglyphic writing appeared. One of its first big jobs was running the state: recording harvests, taxes and stores,

20
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:29,400
so one king could govern a country hundreds of kilometres long. Below the king, a chief minister, the vizier,

21
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:36,600
ran the government, and the country was divided into about 40 provinces, each with its own governor.

22
00:02:36,600 --> 00:02:43,800
Remember those governors. Egyptians worshipped many gods tied to the world around them: Ra, the sun; Osiris,

23
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:51,500
god of the dead and of rebirth, linked to the flood's yearly return; and his wife, Isis. These gods would last for 3,000 years.

24
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:59,700
The king wasn't just a ruler. Egyptians believed he was a god on earth, whose job was to keep Ma'at, the order of the universe,

25
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:06,600
so the sun rose and the river flooded on time. That made him all-powerful, as long as the river behaved.

26
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:14,800
Egyptians believed the king's spirit lived on after death, and that his afterlife helped keep the whole country in order.

27
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:21,500
So his tomb mattered to everyone. Early kings were buried under flat mudbrick tombs called mastabas.

28
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:27,700
Around 2650 BC, King Djoser's official Imhotep built his tomb in stone instead,

29
00:03:27,700 --> 00:03:34,900
stacking six layers about 62 metres high. The Step Pyramid is the oldest large cut-stone building in the world,

30
00:03:34,900 --> 00:03:43,600
and it started the age of pyramids. The next king, Sneferu, struggled to get the shape right. His Bent Pyramid changes angle halfway up,

31
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:51,500
most likely because the first slope was too steep. His Red Pyramid finally got it right: the first true smooth-sided pyramid.

32
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:57,000
His son Khufu built the Great Pyramid of Giza around 2560 BC:

33
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:04,900
about 2.3 million stone blocks, 146 metres tall when finished. Nothing built by humans stood taller

34
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:11,800
for about 3,800 years. Deep inside, narrow passages lead to the king's granite burial chamber.

35
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:19,500
By the time explorers reached it, it was empty. The river made it possible. Most of the stone was cut right at Giza,

36
00:04:19,500 --> 00:04:26,400
but fine white limestone for the outer casing came by boat from quarries across the river, and huge granite beams

37
00:04:26,400 --> 00:04:34,100
for the king's burial chamber came from Aswan, hundreds of kilometres upstream. How they raised the blocks is still debated.

38
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:42,300
The most likely answer is ramps of mudbrick and rubble, with blocks dragged on wooden sledges. Wetting the sand in front of a sledge

39
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:49,700
cuts the friction sharply, and an Egyptian tomb painting shows a worker doing exactly that. It was not built by slaves.

40
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:58,100
Archaeologists found the builders' town beside the pyramids, with bakeries, breweries and sleeping halls, and the workers' own tombs nearby.

41
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:06,000
Many were farmers working in rotating shifts, likely during the flood season, when their fields were under water anyway.

42
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:15,200
We even have a logbook from one of the crews. In 2013, archaeologists found the oldest inscribed papyri ever discovered,

43
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:21,400
including the diary of an inspector named Merer. It records his team ferrying limestone

44
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:29,300
from the quarries at Tura to a harbour at Giza, trip after trip, in the reign of Khufu. A pyramid wasn't just a tomb.

45
00:05:29,300 --> 00:05:36,500
Each came with temples, a causeway and farmland whose harvests paid priests to make offerings to the dead king forever.

46
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:42,700
Over generations, more and more of Egypt's land and grain went to supporting dead kings and their priests.

47
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:51,100
Khufu's son Khafre built the second pyramid at Giza, and the Great Sphinx was most likely carved in his reign. The third, built by Menkaure,

48
00:05:51,100 --> 00:05:59,000
is far smaller. After Giza, pyramids kept shrinking. That tells you something. The kings were spending less, while the officials,

49
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:05,900
priests and provincial governors who ran the state were growing richer and more independent. Late in this era,

50
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:11,600
kings began carving spells on their burial chamber walls to help them rise to the sky after death.

51
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:20,300
These Pyramid Texts are among the oldest religious writings in the world. For about 500 years, Egypt's god-kings built mountains of stone.

52
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:28,200
Then the river stopped cooperating. Around 2200 BC, the climate shifted and the floods shrank, year after year.

53
00:06:28,200 --> 00:06:35,400
Harvests failed and people starved. And a god-king who couldn't make the river rise no longer looked much like a god.

54
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:43,600
Pepi II, the last great king of this era, may have reigned for more than 60 years. By then, provincial governors had made

55
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:50,300
their posts hereditary and were ruling like independent kings. After him, central power collapsed.

56
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:58,000
For more than a century, there was no single Egypt. Rival dynasties ruled from the north and from Thebes in the south,

57
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:05,700
local strongmen fought each other, and famine hit village after village. Historians call it the First Intermediate Period.

58
00:07:05,700 --> 00:07:12,400
Something lasting came out of it, too. With the kings weakened, wealthy local families began claiming

59
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:20,600
an afterlife that had once belonged to the king, painting the old royal spells inside their own coffins. Around 2050 BC,

60
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:29,000
Mentuhotep II of Thebes defeated the northern dynasty and reunited the country. This began the Middle Kingdom.

61
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:36,700
Its kings had learned from the collapse. They moved the capital north, near the Faiyum oasis, drained its marshes to grow more food,

62
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:44,600
cut the power of the provincial governors, and built a chain of massive forts along the Nile in Nubia to guard the south.

63
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:51,500
For about 300 years, Egypt was stable again. It was also Egypt's classic age of literature.

64
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:59,400
Stories and teachings written in this period were copied in schools for centuries afterwards. But after about two centuries,

65
00:07:59,400 --> 00:08:06,300
the royal line weakened. Dozens of short-lived kings followed one another, and their grip on the north slipped.

66
00:08:06,300 --> 00:08:13,500
Meanwhile, settlers from the Levant had been moving into the northern delta. As the Middle Kingdom weakened,

67
00:08:13,500 --> 00:08:20,200
their leaders took control of the north, around 1650 BC, ruling from their capital, Avaris.

68
00:08:20,200 --> 00:08:27,900
Egyptians called them "rulers of foreign lands." We call them the Hyksos. They had horse-drawn chariots, powerful composite bows

69
00:08:27,900 --> 00:08:35,800
and better bronze weapons, which Egypt had barely used before. For the first time, foreigners ruled the land of the pharaohs,

70
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:43,500
and the Egyptian kings in Thebes had to accept them. Yet the Hyksos didn't try to erase Egypt. They took pharaohs' titles,

71
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:50,900
wrote their names in hieroglyphs, and worshipped Egyptian gods alongside their own. It's the first time we see a pattern

72
00:08:50,900 --> 00:08:58,300
that runs through this whole story: foreign rulers turning themselves into Egyptian kings. The kings of Thebes fought back.

73
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:05,200
Seqenenre died in battle, and his mummy's skull still shows the axe and spear wounds. Thebes was squeezed from both sides,

74
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:13,600
because the Hyksos were in contact with the kingdom of Kush to the south. The next Theban king, Kamose, intercepted a Hyksos letter

75
00:09:13,600 --> 00:09:21,000
urging Kush to attack Thebes while his army was away. The Thebans adopted the enemy's chariots and bows,

76
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:28,400
and around 1550 BC, Ahmose captured Avaris, drove the Hyksos out, and chased them into Canaan.

77
00:09:28,400 --> 00:09:36,600
Foreign rule changed Egypt. It now had a professional army, and it had learned that the deserts were no longer enough.

78
00:09:36,600 --> 00:09:43,500
From now on, it would push its borders outward and build an empire. Ahmose's victory began the New Kingdom,

79
00:09:43,500 --> 00:09:51,700
Egypt's richest era. Thebes became its religious heart, and its god Amun, merged with the sun god Ra, became king of the gods.

80
00:09:51,700 --> 00:09:59,400
As the empire grew, his temple at Karnak received a large share of the plunder, and his priests became enormously rich.

81
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:06,300
The new pharaohs went on the attack. Thutmose I led his army all the way to the Euphrates, in Syria,

82
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:14,500
and deep into Nubia in the south. Almost every pyramid had been robbed by now, so the new pharaohs hid their tombs instead,

83
00:10:14,500 --> 00:10:21,900
cutting them deep into a desert valley near Thebes: the Valley of the Kings. Around 1479 BC,

84
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:29,100
the child Thutmose III became king, and his aunt and stepmother, Hatshepsut, ruled as regent. Within a few years,

85
00:10:29,100 --> 00:10:37,000
she declared herself pharaoh, and her official images showed her with a king's headdress and the ceremonial false beard.

86
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:44,200
To justify her rule, she had her temple walls carved with scenes showing the god Amun as her real father.

87
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:52,600
She ruled for about 20 years, mostly through trade and building rather than war. Her expedition to Punt, on Africa's Red Sea coast,

88
00:10:52,600 --> 00:10:59,300
brought back incense, ebony and living myrrh trees, and her terraced temple at Deir el-Bahari still stands.

89
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:07,200
She also raised giant granite obelisks at Karnak. Years after her death, her statues were smashed and her name was chiselled

90
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:14,400
off monuments, most likely on Thutmose III's orders. Later official king lists left her out entirely.

91
00:11:14,400 --> 00:11:21,600
Once he ruled alone, Thutmose III became Egypt's greatest conqueror. Around 1457 BC,

92
00:11:21,600 --> 00:11:28,800
rebel kings gathered at Megiddo, in what is now northern Israel. Thutmose took the narrowest of three routes,

93
00:11:28,800 --> 00:11:36,500
a pass so tight his army marched in single file, and caught them by surprise. After a seven-month siege, Megiddo fell.

94
00:11:36,500 --> 00:11:43,900
We know these details because Thutmose had his campaigns carved on the walls of Karnak, listing battles, captured cities

95
00:11:43,900 --> 00:11:52,300
and tribute, much of it given to Amun's temple. Over about 17 campaigns, he built the largest empire Egypt ever had,

96
00:11:52,300 --> 00:11:58,300
stretching from Syria to deep into Sudan, with Nubia's gold mines under Egyptian control.

97
00:11:58,300 --> 00:12:05,700
He also found a way to hold it. Sons of conquered rulers were brought to Egypt and raised at court. When they went home

98
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:13,100
to rule, they had grown up Egyptian, and loyal. By the reign of Amenhotep III, Egypt was a superpower,

99
00:12:13,100 --> 00:12:20,000
rich enough to spend almost four decades building rather than fighting. Clay tablets later found at Amarna

100
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:27,900
preserve letters from the kings of Babylon and other great powers, writing to the pharaoh as equals and asking him for gold.

101
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:35,800
Then his son broke the system. Amenhotep IV pushed aside Egypt's many gods for one, the Aten, the disc of the sun,

102
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:44,200
and renamed himself Akhenaten. Many historians see this partly as a move against the rich and powerful priests of Amun.

103
00:12:44,200 --> 00:12:51,600
He built a new capital, now called Amarna, closed the old temples, and had Amun's name hacked off monuments.

104
00:12:51,600 --> 00:12:59,800
Only the king and his queen, Nefertiti, were shown worshipping the Aten directly. Everyone else was meant to worship through them.

105
00:12:59,800 --> 00:13:07,000
Art changed too. The royal family was shown with long faces and rounded bodies, in relaxed family scenes,

106
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:14,200
a sharp break from 1,500 years of stiff, idealised royal images. Meanwhile, the empire drifted.

107
00:13:14,200 --> 00:13:22,400
Among the Amarna letters are pleas from Egypt's vassal rulers in Canaan, begging the pharaoh for troops against their enemies.

108
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:29,800
Some seem to have gone unanswered. His revolution didn't survive him. After his death, Amarna was abandoned,

109
00:13:29,800 --> 00:13:35,800
and Egypt went back to its old gods. The boy who soon took the throne, Tutankhaten, was about nine.

110
00:13:35,800 --> 00:13:44,000
His advisers reopened the old temples and changed his name to honour Amun: Tutankhamun. He died at around 19,

111
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:50,900
and the exact cause is still debated. A few years later, the army commander Horemheb became pharaoh.

112
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:57,800
He tore down Akhenaten's temples, reused their blocks, and wiped the Amarna kings, Tutankhamun included,

113
00:13:57,800 --> 00:14:04,500
from the official record. That's partly why Tutankhamun was forgotten. He had been buried in a small,

114
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:11,400
hastily prepared tomb, and later, workers digging a nearby tomb piled their debris over its entrance.

115
00:14:11,400 --> 00:14:18,800
Unlike almost every other royal tomb, it was never emptied by robbers. In November 1922,

116
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:26,000
British archaeologist Howard Carter uncovered its steps and sealed doorway. When his sponsor, Lord Carnarvon,

117
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:33,700
asked if he could see anything inside, Carter answered: "Yes, wonderful things." Inside were more than 5,000 objects.

118
00:14:33,700 --> 00:14:40,600
The king lay in three nested coffins, the innermost made of solid gold, about 110 kilograms,

119
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:48,500
with the famous gold mask over his face. And this was the burial of a minor king, which tells you how much was lost from the others.

120
00:14:48,500 --> 00:14:57,700
The discovery made Tutankhamun the most famous pharaoh in the world. It came in the same year Egypt won formal independence from Britain,

121
00:14:57,700 --> 00:15:04,200
and Egyptians embraced the boy king as a symbol of their nation. The treasures stayed in Egypt.

122
00:15:04,200 --> 00:15:11,100
Horemheb had no heir, so he passed the throne to another general, Ramesses I. His family,

123
00:15:11,100 --> 00:15:19,500
the Ramessides, were soldiers from the delta, and they set out to rebuild Egypt's empire. The most famous was Ramesses II,

124
00:15:19,500 --> 00:15:27,900
who reigned for 66 years. He built a new capital, Pi-Ramesses, in the eastern delta, closer to the frontier in Asia,

125
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:35,600
and filled Egypt with temples and giant statues of himself, including the four colossal figures at Abu Simbel.

126
00:15:35,600 --> 00:15:42,100
Later Egyptians remembered him as the greatest king of all, and nine later pharaohs took his name.

127
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:48,100
In 1274 BC, he fought the Hittite empire for control of Syria at Kadesh,

128
00:15:48,100 --> 00:15:55,500
in one of the largest chariot battles in history. Ramesses was caught in an ambush and had to fight his way out.

129
00:15:55,500 --> 00:16:02,400
The battle ended without a clear winner, though Ramesses advertised it at home as a great victory.

130
00:16:02,400 --> 00:16:09,100
About 15 years later, the two empires signed one of the earliest peace treaties whose text survives,

131
00:16:09,100 --> 00:16:18,000
promising to help each other against attackers. Ramesses later married a Hittite princess to seal the peace. Around 1200 BC,

132
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:25,200
the Bronze Age world collapsed. The Hittite empire fell, and cities across the eastern Mediterranean were destroyed.

133
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:33,400
Under Ramesses III, Egypt beat back invaders it called the Sea Peoples, but war, failing trade and poor harvests drained it.

134
00:16:33,400 --> 00:16:41,100
Egypt soon lost its lands in Canaan and Syria. The state began to fail at home. Around 1157 BC,

135
00:16:41,100 --> 00:16:49,500
royal tomb builders went unpaid and staged the first recorded strike in history. Within decades, officials were putting gangs on trial

136
00:16:49,500 --> 00:16:56,700
for robbing the royal tombs in the Valley of the Kings. To save what was left, priests gathered the mummies of great pharaohs

137
00:16:56,700 --> 00:17:04,400
and hid them together in secret caches. That's why we can still see the faces of kings like Ramesses II today.

138
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:11,600
By about 1070 BC, the New Kingdom was over. The high priests of Amun ruled the south from Thebes,

139
00:17:11,600 --> 00:17:20,800
while separate kings ruled the delta. Power slipped to outsiders. Families of Libyan descent, settled in Egypt for generations,

140
00:17:20,800 --> 00:17:27,700
took the throne. Then kings from Kush, in today's Sudan, marched north. Around 727 BC,

141
00:17:27,700 --> 00:17:36,400
King Piye conquered Egypt, and his family ruled as pharaohs for more than half a century, restoring old temples and traditions.

142
00:17:36,400 --> 00:17:42,600
Next came the Assyrian empire. In 671 BC, its army took Memphis,

143
00:17:42,600 --> 00:17:51,000
and in 663 BC it sacked Thebes and drove the Kushite kings back south for good. A delta prince, Psamtik I,

144
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:58,900
used the Assyrians' retreat and hired Greek soldiers to reunite Egypt. His dynasty was the last great native revival,

145
00:17:58,900 --> 00:18:04,600
and it lasted about 140 years. It opened Egypt to Greek traders,

146
00:18:04,600 --> 00:18:13,300
and Psamtik's son Necho II tried to win back Syria. But in 605 BC, the Babylonian prince Nebuchadnezzar

147
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:21,200
crushed the Egyptian army at Carchemish. Egypt's days as an empire were over. In 525 BC,

148
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:28,400
the Persian king Cambyses defeated the last king of that dynasty at Pelusium, on Egypt's eastern border.

149
00:18:28,400 --> 00:18:36,300
Egypt became a province of the largest empire the world had yet seen, ruled from Iran. Some Persian kings, like Darius I,

150
00:18:36,300 --> 00:18:44,700
respected Egyptian temples and ruled through local officials. Others were harsher, and Egyptians rebelled again and again.

151
00:18:44,700 --> 00:18:50,900
In 404 BC, they finally broke free. Independence lasted about 60 years.

152
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:59,100
In 343 BC, Persia invaded again and won. The pharaoh who lost that war, Nectanebo II,

153
00:18:59,100 --> 00:19:06,300
is usually counted as the last native Egyptian to rule Egypt for about 2,300 years.

154
00:19:06,300 --> 00:19:14,700
That's the number from the start of this video. In 332 BC, Alexander the Great took Egypt from the Persians,

155
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:22,400
and many Egyptians welcomed him. He travelled to the oracle of Amun at Siwa, in the western desert,

156
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:30,100
and was hailed as the god's son. Egypt accepted him as pharaoh. He founded a new city on the coast: Alexandria.

157
00:19:30,100 --> 00:19:37,000
He left after a few months and never returned alive, but his body was later brought back and buried there.

158
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:43,700
After Alexander's death in 323 BC, his general Ptolemy took Egypt,

159
00:19:43,700 --> 00:19:51,600
and Ptolemy's family ruled it for nearly 300 years. The system was simple: Greek-speaking officials at the top,

160
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:59,500
Egyptian farmers at the bottom, and the Nile's harvest taxed to fund the court. To Egyptians, the Ptolemies presented

161
00:19:59,500 --> 00:20:06,400
themselves as pharaohs. They built huge temples in the traditional style, like those at Edfu and Dendera,

162
00:20:06,400 --> 00:20:13,600
with their Greek names carved in hieroglyphs. Foreign rulers kept becoming, at least on temple walls, Egyptian kings.

163
00:20:13,600 --> 00:20:21,500
Alexandria became the greatest city of the Greek world, with its lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders, and its Great Library.

164
00:20:21,500 --> 00:20:28,700
Scholars there worked out the size of the Earth and wrote the geometry still taught today. But the royal court itself spoke Greek.

165
00:20:28,700 --> 00:20:35,900
The Ptolemies kept power in the family, often by brothers marrying sisters. Heavy taxes bred resentment,

166
00:20:35,900 --> 00:20:43,800
and for about 20 years, rebels held much of southern Egypt. In 196 BC, during that revolt,

167
00:20:43,800 --> 00:20:50,500
priests at Memphis issued a decree honouring the young king Ptolemy V. It was carved in three scripts:

168
00:20:50,500 --> 00:20:57,400
hieroglyphs, the everyday Egyptian script called Demotic, and Greek. Remember this stone. The last ruler

169
00:20:57,400 --> 00:21:05,300
of the dynasty was Cleopatra VII. According to the ancient writer Plutarch, she was the first of her family

170
00:21:05,300 --> 00:21:13,000
to learn Egyptian. She needed powerful allies to keep Egypt free of Rome, which was swallowing the Mediterranean.

171
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:20,400
In 48 BC, she was fighting her own brother for the throne when Julius Caesar arrived in Alexandria.

172
00:21:20,400 --> 00:21:27,300
She won his support, he defeated her rivals, and she had a son with him, Caesarion. After Caesar's murder,

